#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.12"
# dependencies = []
# ///
"""Check that ``ledger/assertions.csv`` covers every upstream VEP assertion (#110).

::

    tools/check_ledger [--csv PATH] [--upstream DIR] [--ref REF]
                       [--list | --sweep | --sweep-dir DIR] [--glob GLOB]

Default mode checks the CSV (default ``ledger/assertions.csv``, schema of #109):

(i) schema: the 13 header names of #109, optionally followed by exactly one
    ``data_test_verdict`` column (#101); field counts, enums, integer columns,
    conditional columns, ``issue`` empty or ``https://``, no newline in a field,
    rows sorted by ``vep_file`` (byte order) then ``n`` (numeric), unique key,
    and file bytes equal to their canonical RFC 4180 re-serialisation (LF, no BOM);
(ii) coverage: the upstream assertions of the 49 ``t/*.t`` of Ensembl VEP
    ``release/116.0`` are enumerated and compared two-way with the CSV keys
    ``(vep_file, n)``: missing rows, orphan rows, ``perl_line`` / ``perl_kind``
    mismatches. ``data_test_verdict`` is never compared.

The enumerator is a line-start regex over 22 assertion function names (#110
Q2 = iii): a line is a row if it matches ``^\\s*(<name>)\\b``; ``n`` is the ordinal
in file order. It is correct for these 49 files at the pinned commit only, which
is why any other commit is refused (exit 2).

The upstream checkout (#110 Q1 = B) is a partial sparse clone of the pinned tag
into a temp dir, or ``--upstream DIR``; either way ``git rev-parse HEAD`` must be
the pinned commit, ``git status --porcelain`` must be empty, and the files the
glob selects on disk must equal those in ``git ls-tree -r HEAD`` (a sparse
checkout that omits a file is refused), none may be flagged assume-unchanged or
skip-worktree in the index, and the bytes the tool reads must hash (in Python, not
through git) to the pinned blob ids, raw or after the ``core.autocrlf=true`` CRLF to
LF conversion (#184): a checkout whose own config (``core.worktree``,
``core.fsmonitor``) or index flags hide an edit from ``git status`` is refused;
every git call runs with ``--no-replace-objects``, so a replace ref cannot swap
the pinned tree. ``--ref`` only selects the tag to clone;
the pin never changes.

``--list`` prints the enumeration as ``vep_file<TAB>n<TAB>perl_line<TAB>perl_kind``.
``--sweep`` (iii) searches the files for every documented function name of the four
test modules as a bare word, at line start or mid-line, after removing comments,
strings, regex literals, sigiled names (``$ @ % &``, so ``&ok(...)`` is not seen),
every ``{ word }`` (so ``{ fail }`` and ``eval { pass }`` are not seen) and
``word =>``,
and prints every occurrence the 22-name rule did not count and no fixed rule
explains, as ``file:line: uncounted `name`: text``; explained occurrences are
tallied on the stderr summary line. ``--sweep-dir DIR`` runs the sweep on ``DIR``
without the pin check (for fixtures only; the summary says ``unpinned``).
``--glob`` (default ``t/*.t``) selects the files for ``--list`` and the sweeps.

Exit codes: ``0`` ok; ``1`` violations (one ``file:n: reason`` line each on stdout);
``2`` cannot measure (CSV unreadable or a field over 131072 bytes, upstream
unavailable, not the pinned commit, dirty upstream tree, ``--upstream`` is a
subdirectory rather than the work-tree top level, the glob selects no file of the
pinned tree, file set differs from the pinned tree, a selected file is flagged
assume-unchanged or skip-worktree, file content differs from the pinned blobs, git
missing). The check summary
``rows R, files F, missing M, orphan O`` is the last stdout line of the default mode.
"""

